    "case_summary": "A 39-year-old female teacher from Southwest China presented with a subcutaneous mass in the lower-left abdominal region. The patient had a history of chronic urticaria (relieved by occasional use of loratadine), cesarean section eight years ago, and myomectomy to remove a myoma six months ago. She denied a history of dysmenorrhea and abortion. The mass was accompanied by slight pain that worsened around menstruation.\n\nHeart, lungs, and abdomen showed no obvious abnormalities. No palpable swelling of superficial lymph nodes was noted. A subcutaneous nodule of approximately 1 cm diameter was present under the hypogastrium. The nodule's surface was normal, without ulceration or bleeding. A linear scar was observed in the lower-left abdominal region.\n\n- Ultrasonography:    Image Title: Ultrasonic image    Image Description: Showed a hybrid echo-mass about 1.1 × 1.0 cm, with an irregular shape, well-defined border, located 3 mm from the body surface in the subcutaneous soft tissue    - Histopathology:    Image Title: Hematoxylin-eosin staining (×40 and ×200)    Image Description: Revealed endometrial glands consisting of a single layer of monomorphous cuboidal cells with an endometrial stroma",
